Configuration resources: the served schema beside every value, a change
typed against it, and a confirmed change driven to its end (RFC 05 §5.1).

```console
$ zenctl config --help
Configuration resources on the `@rpc` plane (RFC 05 §5.1).

Read the served schema beside every running value, change one group typed against it, and drive a confirmed change to its end.

Usage: zenctl config <COMMAND>

Commands:
  get      Read a resource's configuration: the served schema beside every running value, its source, and any pending change (on-bus)
  set      Change one group of a resource, typed against the served schema (on-bus)
  confirm  Make a pending change permanent (on-bus)
  cancel   Undo a pending change now (on-bus)
  extend   Move a pending change's deadline (on-bus)
  persist  Write a confirmed change into the producer's persisted layer — its own key, so an ACL grants it apart from the change (on-bus)
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl config get --help
Read a resource's configuration: the served schema beside every running value, its source, and any pending change (on-bus)

Usage: zenctl config get [OPTIONS] <ORIGIN> <PRODUCER> <RESOURCE>

Arguments:
  <ORIGIN>
          Origin to target: a host id (`h-3fa9c2d41b7e`) or `*` for the fleet

  <PRODUCER>
          Producer name

  <RESOURCE>
          The resource — the chunk an ACL grants by: a device, an interface

Options:
      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl config set --help
Change one group of a resource, typed against the served schema (on-bus).

The read-back is fetched first, so a value is read as the kind the producer declares and refused here — in the producer's own words — when the producer would refuse it. A `reach` group needs `--confirm` and a yes; a `contract` group is refused with the restart named.

Usage: zenctl config set [OPTIONS] <ORIGIN> <PRODUCER> <RESOURCE> <GROUP> <NAME=VALUE>...

Arguments:
  <ORIGIN>
          Origin to target: one host id — a change never fans out

  <PRODUCER>
          Producer name

  <RESOURCE>
          The resource the group belongs to

  <GROUP>
          The group to change — the unit that has a class and that the write key names

  <NAME=VALUE>...
          The values, `name=value`, one or more; a subset of the group changes those alone

Options:
      --dry-run
          Validate and report what would change, without touching the device

      --confirm <SECS>
          Arm a rollback: the change is undone after SECS unless confirmed. Required for a `reach` group

      --expect-revision <N>
          Refuse the change if the document's revision has moved past this

      --idempotency-key <KEY>
          A key a retry carries, so a lost reply is not a doubled write

      --actor <NAME>
          Who is asking, for the change event's record — a claimed label, never an authentication (RFC 06 §5.5)

      --request-id <ID>
          A request id for the change event's record, likewise claimed

      --yes
          Send a `reach` change without being asked (for a script that has decided)

      --no-validate
          Skip the read-back: values ride by their spelling and the producer judges the rest

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.{toml,kdl}`), repeatable. Joined with the live bus as a union (RFC 08 §6.1): a producer's served slice wins, these files fill the gaps, and a disagreement is reported — never silently overwritten. With the bus unreachable they answer alone

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Reply-wait only: a passive observation window is `--for`

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

